<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Din Eidyn</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Eidyn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Din Eidyn was the Brittonic name of the fortress on the rock of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, the chief stronghold of the post-Roman kingdom of Gododdin. The Welsh poem Y Gododdin describes its war band feasting there before the doomed attack on Catraeth. The Northumbrians besieged it in 638, and it later passed into English and then Scottish hands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/din-eidyn.jpg" alt="Din Eidyn" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: myself · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yr.Hen.Ogledd.550.650.Koch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
